WEBVTT

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:09.918
A machine of the lab can also go to libvirt, to keep using it in virt-manager. This small Alpine server is installed and stopped.

00:00:10.018 --> 00:00:20.950
export-libvirt with copy-to-pool copies its disk into a libvirt pool: an independent machine, vmctl's own disk stays as it was.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:25.534
libvirt knows it now.

00:00:25.634 --> 00:00:58.101
It starts like any libvirt machine, and virt-manager shows its console.

00:00:58.201 --> 00:01:07.018
Shut it down from libvirt.

00:01:07.118 --> 00:01:16.098
Say you remove it from vmctl: clean deletes vmctl's disk, but keeps the SSH keys the copy knows.

00:01:16.198 --> 00:01:25.114
The way back: unexport-libvirt downloads the copy as vmctl's disk, puts the keys back, and removes it from libvirt.

00:01:25.214 --> 00:02:03.152
It is vmctl's machine again: it starts…

00:02:03.252 --> 00:02:08.306
…and answers over SSH, with the same key as before.

00:02:08.406 --> 00:02:19.874
Without copy-to-pool the export is linked instead: libvirt runs vmctl's own disk, and unexport hands it back.
